<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zu Chongzhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/429">429</a>–500</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zu Chongzhi was a Chinese mathematician and astronomer of the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Southern Qi dynasties. He calculated pi to lie between 3.1415926 and 3.1415927, a precision not surpassed for nearly a thousand years, and gave 355/113 as a close approximation. His Daming calendar of 462, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to take account of the precession of the equinoxes, was adopted in 510.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zu-chongzhi.jpg" alt="Zu Chongzhi" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 三猎 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%A5%96%E5%86%B2%E4%B9%8B%E9%93%9C%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zu Chongzhi, courtesy name Wenyuan, came from a family that originated in what is now Baoding, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>. His grandfather Zu Chang moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> during the mass population movement of the Eastern Jin and later held the post of Chief Minister for the Palace Buildings under the Liu Song, directing government construction. His father Zu Shuozhi also served the court and was respected for his learning.</p><p>Zu was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>. His family had long been involved in astronomical research, so he met both astronomy and mathematics from childhood, and his talent won him a reputation while he was still a youth. Emperor Xiaowu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> sent him to the Hualin Xuesheng academy and later to the Imperial Nanjing University, known as Zongmingguan, to carry out research.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 461 Zu worked in the office of the local governor at Nanxu, today Zhenjiang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>. In 464 he moved to Louxian, today the Songjiang district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, where he compiled the Daming calendar and calculated pi. In 488 he was responsible for erecting water-powered trip hammer mills, which Emperor Wu of Southern Qi inspected in the early 490s.</p><p>He is also credited with the Chinese paddle boat, or Qianli chuan, in the late 5th century, and many paddle-wheel ships were built in the Tang era. In 478 he reconstructed the south-pointing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, first made by Ma Jun. The Book of Qi says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Emperor Wu of Liu Song</a> had captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-xing">Yao Xing</a>'s carriage as an empty shell with no machinery, so a man inside had to turn the figure, and Zu made new bronze machinery that turned without a hitch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Zu used Liu Hui's algorithm, inscribing a polygon of 12,288 sides, and bounded pi between 3.1415926 and 3.1415927, a precision that stood for centuries. His other notable approximations were 355/113, called milü or the close ratio, and 22/7, the yuelü. The Japanese mathematician Yoshio Mikami noted that 355/113 appears in no Greek, Indian or Arabian manuscript before 1585 and urged that it be called Zu's fraction. He and his son Zu Gengzhi also derived the volume of a sphere as πd³/6.</p><p>In astronomy he distinguished the sidereal year from the tropical year, and calculated the year as 365.24281481 days. He gave the Jupiter year as about 11.858 Earth years. With his son he wrote the mathematical text Zhui Shu, which was lost after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, and his book Accounts of Strange Things survives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zu Chongzhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The astronomer <b>Zu Chongzhi</b>, who lived in the Liu Song period, is noted for calculating pi to seven decimal places.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liu-song">Liu Song · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in China. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.06,118.8&amp;year=475" aria-label="Jiankang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7607 997 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zu Chongzhi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8057 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8057 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.06,118.8&amp;year=475">What was here in 475 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
